62
00:11:45.630 --> 00:11:53.120
Thomas Kerber: I don't think, actually, it's specifically an issue for… elliptic curves,

63
00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:58.970
Thomas Kerber: For elliptic curves, there's an additional confusion, which is that, there's two…

64
00:11:59.510 --> 00:12:03.600
Thomas Kerber: Common notations for elliptic curves, multiplicative and additive notation.

65
00:12:04.150 --> 00:12:05.720
Thomas Kerber: And this…

66
00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:18.389
Thomas Kerber: Well, we kind of… actually, because of the way we've named library functions, we're kind of already implicitly using the additive notation, but this would bake us more into that, and it would make it hard to change in the future.

67
00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:24.410
Thomas Kerber: Which isn't necessarily a point against it, it's just a…

68
00:12:25.470 --> 00:12:36.649
Thomas Kerber: Oh, it's a complexity, and the other side of that is… one of the oddities is it means that the multiply operation… the multiplication operand

69
00:12:36.820 --> 00:12:47.199
Thomas Kerber: is not… It uses two different types for elliptic curves, which is a bit of an odd case.

70
00:12:49.110 --> 00:12:50.780
Kent Dybvig: That's currently the case.

71
00:12:51.810 --> 00:12:56.319
Thomas Kerber: Yeah, so if you look at the signature for ECMOL, it is not.

72
00:12:56.320 --> 00:12:57.299
Kevin Millikin: by a scaler.

73
00:12:57.490 --> 00:12:58.060
Kevin Millikin: It's multiple…

74
00:12:58.060 --> 00:12:58.940
Kent Dybvig: Right, yeah.

75
00:12:59.410 --> 00:13:00.859
Kent Dybvig: So it's not translate.

76
00:13:01.410 --> 00:13:03.040
Kent Dybvig: Not trans, I'm not…

77
00:13:03.420 --> 00:13:04.630
Kevin Millikin: commutative.

78
00:13:04.630 --> 00:13:06.070
Kent Dybvig: I mean, to the face, sorry.

79
00:13:06.820 --> 00:13:07.940
Kent Dybvig: It's been a long time.

80
00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:21.769
Thomas Kerber: Yeah, you get into all sorts of odd questions, like, do you allow writing, B times A, because… instead of A times B, because it still has

81
00:13:22.360 --> 00:13:29.110
Thomas Kerber: a mathematical interpretation. You can still say that they're both defined, but you can't say that

82
00:13:29.340 --> 00:13:34.600
Thomas Kerber: A times B times C is well-defined, or, you know, then you start getting into problems.

83
00:13:34.940 --> 00:13:39.920
Thomas Kerber: Yeah, it just opens a bit of a can of worms, which…

84
00:13:40.590 --> 00:13:42.449
Thomas Kerber: We will have to think about that.

85
00:13:43.140 --> 00:13:48.830
Kevin Millikin: Yeah, I do think that's a great point, Thomas, that people expect certain properties of arithmetic.

86
00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:54.230
Kevin Millikin: Like, commutativity and associativity, to behave… to behave, and then if you define your own.

87
00:13:54.940 --> 00:13:56.910
Kevin Millikin: They don't necessarily have those properties.

88
00:13:58.370 --> 00:14:06.549
Seba: Additionally, like, concepts like casting don't really work the way people might expect, because you have to, like, catch the curves.

89
00:14:06.740 --> 00:14:08.429
Kent Dybvig: Otherwise, you might be, like.

90
00:14:08.680 --> 00:14:18.799
Seba: Subtly have some series of values where, there's, like, values that don't work, because the, scalar field size might be different for different curves.

