Kevin Millikin: I would say that languages generally take two positions on such things. One is that there are guidelines that you ought to follow for your overloaded operators, but there's nothing that requires you to do that. So you can do some really obscene things with C++ overloading, where the operators have a completely different interpretation than you would expect, because they're just convenient.

142
00:20:28.850 --> 00:20:32.509
Kevin Millikin: Or there's something that the language tries to… to enforce.

143
00:20:32.840 --> 00:20:37.200
Kevin Millikin: Which requires more… The heavyweight signatures, and we're checking.

144
00:20:43.220 --> 00:20:53.629
Kevin Millikin: I was just gonna say that the overflow behavior of uint addition will also potentially, change when we add big uints that can be bigger than the field size.

145
00:20:54.080 --> 00:21:00.989
