Kent Dybvig: So I… specifically, I… these tickets are in a different…

181
00:24:22.370 --> 00:24:27.290
Kent Dybvig: they're not in MNJS, they're not in our, sorry, Midnight Languages.

182
00:24:27.400 --> 00:24:32.249
Kent Dybvig: I'm wondering if we should make them more visible to us in…

183
00:24:32.360 --> 00:24:33.520
Kevin Millikin: Yep, Jura.

184
00:24:34.290 --> 00:24:38.690
Kent Dybvig: maybe either with parallel stories, or…

185
00:24:39.060 --> 00:24:42.109
Kent Dybvig: Or with one story that has a link to it, or…

186
00:24:42.860 --> 00:24:47.079
Kent Dybvig: I mean, I'd like to have it on my radar. I'd like to… you know, I was thinking today.

187
00:24:47.280 --> 00:24:53.150
Kent Dybvig: You know, actually, we were talking with Reece a couple days ago, that we…

188
00:24:53.360 --> 00:25:01.819
Kent Dybvig: We have these things to do, we have to schedule them, and we had to be a little careful how we schedule ones that are disruptive to the shape of the codebase.

189
00:25:01.940 --> 00:25:06.029
Kent Dybvig: So that we're doing it in a period of relative quiet.

190
00:25:09.240 --> 00:25:14.420
Kevin Millikin: Yeah. So I think that there's a… there's a bigger problem there, which is that we're getting,

191
00:25:15.000 --> 00:25:30.669
Kevin Millikin: as… we're using Jira for more things, which means there's more different teams, there's more different kinds of ways of tagging issues, and I have also experienced that there are issues that I know exist, but I can't find them, because I don't know how they're tagged, or how to search for them.

192
00:25:31.700 --> 00:25:40.100
Kevin Millikin: One thing we could do is, I think we can make really liberal use of tagging JIRA issues, because tags are relatively freeform, and anyone can create them.

193
00:25:40.250 --> 00:25:49.440
Kevin Millikin: So we could, instead of filtering by team, or filtering by initiatives, we could also just tag issues that were of interest for some reason.

194
00:25:49.700 --> 00:25:51.869
Andrey Venuto: tag, as in label, kind of thing?

195
00:25:52.260 --> 00:26:10.059
Kevin Millikin: Yeah, just a label on the issue. Like, GitHub has tags, and you can make them colored, and you can, you can just freely create them, so we could have our own system of tags that we used for things. I was… I filed an issue for the formatter today, and there's no component for the formatter.

196
00:26:10.250 --> 00:26:12.270
Kevin Millikin: And I can't… I don't know how to create a component.

197
00:26:12.580 --> 00:26:14.999
Kevin Millikin: And I don't know if there should be more components.

198
00:26:15.110 --> 00:26:19.610
Kevin Millikin: But it just would make sense to just tag it as what component.

199
00:26:27.530 --> 00:26:31.589
Kent Dybvig: We're tagging the team as our team, yeah.

200
00:26:32.790 --> 00:26:33.460
Kevin Millikin: Right. Small team.

201
00:26:33.460 --> 00:26:41.629
Kent Dybvig: And we're all doing this, you know, our team is going to be doing this and that and the other thing, and for all those things, it's nice to have the experience all in one place.

202
00:26:42.130 --> 00:26:44.590
Kent Dybvig: Yeah. And I'll have to use different tags to find them.

203
00:26:45.210 --> 00:26:58.720
Kevin Millikin: So I don't know the answer if we can change the team that these audit findings are assigned to, because somebody who wants to look at all the audit findings will now have potentially trouble finding them, so we need to figure that out. But we might be able to change it.

204
00:26:59.700 --> 00:27:00.230
Kent Dybvig: Yeah.

205
00:27:02.520 --> 00:27:07.219
Kent Dybvig: Okay, I guess the same issue relates to anything that shows up as GitHub issues.

206
00:27:09.880 --> 00:27:14.009
Kent Dybvig: They aren't currently being automatically converted to tickets, right?

207
00:27:15.840 --> 00:27:16.390
Kevin Millikin: Nope.

208
00:27:20.140 --> 00:27:26.740
Kevin Millikin: I… at the moment, I don't think that there's any direction, any integration working.

209
00:27:27.050 --> 00:27:27.930
Andrey Venuto: No anymore.

210
00:27:31.880 --> 00:27:32.660
Kent Dybvig: We're not, like.

211
00:27:32.930 --> 00:27:40.219
Kent Dybvig: Might be something to try to get back once we're… once the dust settles on them, move to Linux Foundation, if that's possible.

212
00:27:41.050 --> 00:27:52.639
Kevin Millikin: Yeah. Ideally, my goal would be that when we transfer the repository to the Linux Foundation and open it, then our work happens primarily there, in that repository.

213
00:27:52.990 --> 00:28:01.120
Kevin Millikin: And we need to train our internal, users who are filing JIRA issues to file issues there instead.

214
00:28:01.310 --> 00:28:03.810
Kevin Millikin: Or… and we do that by just…

215
00:28:04.130 --> 00:28:11.439
Kevin Millikin: duplicating the issues ourselves initially. They need to be a little careful that things that they put in those issues

216
00:28:11.760 --> 00:28:12.960
Kevin Millikin: are public.

217
00:28:15.290 --> 00:28:15.900
Kent Dybvig: Yeah.

218
00:28:17.040 --> 00:28:19.380
Kent Dybvig: I'm not… you know…

219
00:28:20.000 --> 00:28:26.309
Kent Dybvig: Not looking for us to do a lot of extra work, but it is nice to have the time bounds

220
00:28:26.870 --> 00:28:29.850
Kent Dybvig: on Jira, like, you don't get in a… in a…

221
00:28:30.200 --> 00:28:32.910
Kent Dybvig: GitHub issues. Issues can just sit there.

222
00:28:33.040 --> 00:28:33.980
Kent Dybvig: And…

223
00:28:35.030 --> 00:28:41.759
Kent Dybvig: Users have no idea how long, you know, we have… we have no idea what's… what's… what's… what each other

224
00:28:42.170 --> 00:28:43.440
Kent Dybvig: are working on.

225
00:28:44.670 --> 00:28:48.810
Kent Dybvig: Anyway… We'll have to come up with a good mess… a good method.

226
00:28:49.050 --> 00:28:56.340
Kent Dybvig: We're coordinating and making sure that the high priority tickets get done, and that we know what we're doing every sprint.

227
00:28:56.890 --> 00:28:57.550
Kevin Millikin: Yep.

228
00:28:57.550 --> 00:28:58.640
Kent Dybvig: If it's not through Jira.

229
00:28:58.640 --> 00:28:59.190
Kevin Millikin: Doubted.

230
00:28:59.500 --> 00:29:10.220
Kevin Millikin: Yeah, there'll be some… there'll be some things that are a little more manual there, but we can create… we can create project boards, and we can put tickets… we can put issues on project boards, and we can move them around.

231
00:29:10.430 --> 00:29:12.390
Kevin Millikin: To reflect different statuses.

232
00:29:12.860 --> 00:29:13.640
Kent Dybvig: Okay.

233
00:29:14.170 --> 00:29:23.360
Kevin Millikin: So we can do some of that. We can do some of that. You still, yeah, you still have some sort of manual triage to make sure that everything is actually in the correct state.

234
00:29:23.590 --> 00:29:25.659
Kevin Millikin: Which we have now, anyway.

235
00:29:27.220 --> 00:29:37.010
Kevin Millikin: Last issue, reserving the TypeScript keywords, any other breaking changes left to make before 1.0? I took a look at the things that we had tagged as breaking changes in Jira, and I don't think there's any more.

236
00:29:40.820 --> 00:29:49.240
Kent Dybvig: Yes, so let… so… The formatting changed, you just…

237
00:29:49.390 --> 00:29:51.550
Kent Dybvig: put forward. Is that a breaking change?

238
00:29:52.090 --> 00:29:56.560
Kent Dybvig: If we change what the formatter produces for… for,

239
00:29:59.570 --> 00:30:04.690
Kent Dybvig: I mean, it could be a breaking change for somebody who's decided that this is a requirement for

240
00:30:05.940 --> 00:30:11.289
Kent Dybvig: Their own project to push to… to their GitHub repo.

241
00:30:11.910 --> 00:30:18.160
Kent Dybvig: Imagine, you know, imagine somebody out there is using our formatter as the standard for what

242
00:30:18.490 --> 00:30:25.780
Kent Dybvig: what you have to push, and… and we change it, we… that changes their… Thing.

243
00:30:26.630 --> 00:30:35.779
Kevin Millikin: Yeah, I mean, that's a good point. I would… I would characterize that as not a breaking change. I would say that the actual requirement is that they require that you have run the formatter before you push to GitHub.

244
00:30:36.640 --> 00:30:37.910
Kevin Millikin: And so…

245
00:30:37.910 --> 00:30:39.469
Kent Dybvig: Say what their requirements are.

246
00:30:39.470 --> 00:30:50.270
Kevin Millikin: No, no, yeah, if they… if they require that the exact formatting… that the exact formatter… formatting that was implemented by 0.1 of the formatter is

247
00:30:51.780 --> 00:30:55.750
Kevin Millikin: is there, then they have to… then they have to actually run that version of the formatter, I guess.

248
00:30:57.270 --> 00:30:58.479
Kent Dybvig: All right, so we won't call that…

249
00:30:58.480 --> 00:30:58.940
Kevin Millikin: It's not…

250
00:30:59.730 --> 00:31:12.730
Kevin Millikin: Yeah, I wouldn't call that a break change. It's not… it's not easy for people to run an older version of the formatter, with a newer toolchain, because they all get updated in lockstep. But I'm not sure how… how much benefit people have from

251
00:31:13.000 --> 00:31:17.579
Kevin Millikin: getting into version confusion, so… I'm pretty happy that…

252
00:31:18.300 --> 00:31:31.090
Kevin Millikin: It's fine. But there are… but it is worth asking, like, there was a discussion about providing some more verbose logging when we compile circuits with the ZKIR binary.

253
00:31:31.540 --> 00:31:34.510
Kevin Millikin: So more information about circuit size.

254
00:31:34.620 --> 00:31:46.649
Kevin Millikin: And then somebody suggested that we should all write this into the contractinfo.json file so that DAPs can see diagnostics or metadata about circuit sizes and do something based on that.

255
00:31:46.920 --> 00:31:49.979
Kevin Millikin: And that would… I mean, that…

256
00:31:50.720 --> 00:32:04.729
Kevin Millikin: is very… that's very different, we don't want to do that. But it occurred to me that even, logging, informational logging, changing that can be a breaking change, because you might have a script.

257
00:32:05.030 --> 00:32:07.539
Kevin Millikin: That parses the logging output.

258
00:32:07.640 --> 00:32:13.960
Kevin Millikin: and does something based on it, and now we've broken you. So we just need to be careful, like, when we communicate those.

259
00:32:14.740 --> 00:32:16.800
Kent Dybvig: Fixing bugs is also a breaking change.

260
00:32:21.990 --> 00:32:31.679
Kent Dybvig: Yeah, just depends on how we define it. You know, what process… how heavyweight our process is, and whether we need to duplicate, you know, we generally won't decide we're going to deprecate

261
00:32:31.820 --> 00:32:33.160
Kent Dybvig: Bucky behavior.

262
00:32:33.510 --> 00:32:36.509
Kent Dybvig: We're just gonna… we're just gonna stop cold turkey on that?

263
00:32:39.390 --> 00:32:55.919
Kent Dybvig: But, on other things, we're gonna have a process, and… and that's just what… what'll pass that bar. Anyway, that's… that's neither here nor there. This question, is… we don't seem to have any other ones. I didn't find any either, but then I have had a lot of trouble finding stories I remembered.

264
00:32:56.840 --> 00:33:00.360
Kent Dybvig: And I, obviously, I'm not finding stories I don't remember.

265
00:33:00.850 --> 00:33:05.890
Kent Dybvig: So… That's why I want to make sure that we aren't missing something.

266
00:33:07.310 --> 00:33:16.000
Kent Dybvig: And then, yeah, we need to reserve the TypeScript keywords, that's the… so that's the last thing. I think we need… we should do that simply because,

267
00:33:16.720 --> 00:33:21.129
Kent Dybvig: the, you know, the generated JavaScript code

268
00:33:21.320 --> 00:33:25.970
Kent Dybvig: If somebody has a circuit name that conflicts with a

269
00:33:26.480 --> 00:33:30.690
Kent Dybvig: TypeScript or JavaScript keyword, it might… it might do funky things.

270
00:33:31.390 --> 00:33:39.259
Kent Dybvig: So, maybe in the generated JavaScript code, it wouldn't do funky things with TypeScript keywords, but in the generated header file, it would.

271
00:33:39.790 --> 00:33:46.690
Kent Dybvig: I think going forward, I would like, control over new keywords to be

272
00:33:48.110 --> 00:33:50.550
Kent Dybvig: Basically via the standard library.

273
00:33:50.810 --> 00:33:54.380
Kent Dybvig: So that people can… Opt out.

274
00:33:54.960 --> 00:33:56.050
Kent Dybvig: easily.

275
00:33:56.180 --> 00:34:05.419
Kent Dybvig: And then I think it's… there's gonna be less pressure on us to deprecate, you know, to have a period where we… where we warn people, or whatever.

276
00:34:09.260 --> 00:34:25.349
Kent Dybvig: So, visibility of new keywords that we would add, such as keywords we might add to allow people to specify new arithmetic types. You know, new… sorry, overloading of plus, if we have a keyword for that.

277
00:34:25.790 --> 00:34:30.419
Kent Dybvig: You know, overload or something like that, then that would be…

278
00:34:30.880 --> 00:34:34.589
Kent Dybvig: Something that you would only get if you imported it from the standard library.

279
00:34:34.710 --> 00:34:40.959
Kent Dybvig: And you could always… Arranged not to import it or to rename it to something else.

280
00:34:44.800 --> 00:34:45.830
Kevin Millikin: Toughness?

281
00:34:46.940 --> 00:34:53.810
Thomas Kerber: Yeah, I was going to ask how having it in the standard library actually helps. I guess, in part, you answer that, because you can…

282
00:34:54.100 --> 00:34:56.610
Thomas Kerber: Choose whether you want to import things or not.

283
00:34:56.770 --> 00:35:00.779
Thomas Kerber: Though that doesn't really help that much with deprecation.

284
00:35:01.030 --> 00:35:05.510
Thomas Kerber: Unless we want to have a way to import old versions of the standard library as well.

285
00:35:07.130 --> 00:35:10.620
Kent Dybvig: No, I don't think it helps with removing old features.

286
00:35:10.930 --> 00:35:16.559
Kent Dybvig: But… So I think my use of the word deprecation was not a very good

287
00:35:16.700 --> 00:35:18.420
Kent Dybvig: choice there, it's just…

288
00:35:19.540 --> 00:35:25.969
Kent Dybvig: There's removing features, which is the deprecation, and that is definitely, something that we're gonna have to do.

289
00:35:26.550 --> 00:35:30.480
Kent Dybvig: in, in steps. But…

290
00:35:30.950 --> 00:35:38.440
Kent Dybvig: for new features, I would like to say that it would be nice if new features we add to the language aren't

291
00:35:38.900 --> 00:35:43.369
Kent Dybvig: aren't… We generally aren't breaking changes, because we…

292
00:35:43.770 --> 00:35:48.239
Kent Dybvig: We can have this mechanism for providing them through the

293
00:35:48.730 --> 00:35:51.780
Kent Dybvig: You know, visibility to them through the standard library.

294
00:35:53.250 --> 00:35:53.940
Thomas Kerber: Right.

295
00:35:54.820 --> 00:35:56.529
Kent Dybvig: Or it could be at the library.

296
00:35:57.600 --> 00:36:02.309
Thomas Kerber: One concern… I have with it is,

297
00:36:03.070 --> 00:36:08.620
Thomas Kerber: Will it limit, uptake of new features because

298
00:36:09.470 --> 00:36:14.800
Thomas Kerber: People have to manually import every single feature that they want to use, then.

299
00:36:15.050 --> 00:36:20.060
Kent Dybvig: No, no, they wouldn't do that. I mean, the point is… It would be, like,

300
00:36:20.220 --> 00:36:29.510
Kent Dybvig: It would be there in the new language version. They'd get an error, but they could… they could… they could, remove it from the… from the import set.

301
00:36:29.510 --> 00:36:37.229
Thomas Kerber: Right. Right. So the path to fixing the conflict would be minimal, because they can just hide it, essentially.

302
00:36:37.940 --> 00:36:43.299
Kent Dybvig: Right, and if they're in the habit, as I think some people will be.

303
00:36:43.440 --> 00:36:50.089
Kent Dybvig: Of explicitly importing only the things that they wish to import, then yes, they would actually have to opt into it.

304
00:36:50.940 --> 00:36:56.419
Kent Dybvig: You know, some people will use the syntax where they only import what they need.

305
00:36:56.600 --> 00:37:02.229
Kent Dybvig: So that they don't clutter their namespace with everything that's in the standard library until they need it.

306
00:37:02.940 --> 00:37:07.689
Kent Dybvig: For those people, they would have to opt into it. For the other people, they'd have to opt out of it.

307
00:37:14.670 --> 00:37:25.220
Kent Dybvig: I don't want to get in a situation where other languages get where they have a fixed set of keywords from day one, and then they have to reuse keywords for 12 different things.

308
00:37:39.350 --> 00:37:43.030
Kent Dybvig: The Shea scheme has literally one reserved word, and that's library.

309
00:37:44.480 --> 00:37:47.170
Kent Dybvig: And it's only reserved at the very top level of your program.

310
00:37:53.490 --> 00:37:59.069
Kevin Millikin: Yeah, I agree in general with that. You get into a syntactic corner that you painted yourself into.

311
00:37:59.840 --> 00:38:01.170
Kevin Millikin: Something easily.

312
00:38:04.570 --> 00:38:06.270
Kevin Millikin: Okay, yeah.

313
00:38:07.330 --> 00:38:12.269
Kevin Millikin: Good. Is there anything else we'd like to talk about while we're all here?

314
00:38:18.360 --> 00:38:20.179
Kevin Millikin: Anything else to add to the agenda?

315
00:38:23.330 --> 00:38:31.030
Kevin Millikin: Okay, if you're not working in the US, be aware that tomorrow and Friday are our company holidays for Shielded.

316
00:38:31.520 --> 00:38:34.509
Kent Dybvig: So U.S.-based employees will be out.

317
00:38:36.600 --> 00:38:40.080
Kevin Millikin: Otherwise, thanks for this. Happy hacking, everybody.

318
00:38:42.240 --> 00:38:42.979
Joseph Denman: Thank you.

319
00:38:43.300 --> 00:38:44.050
Kent Dybvig: Due.

